<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalhora dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1701 CE – 1783 CE (approx.) · capital Khudabad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sindhi dynasty of Sufi descent that ruled the Indus valley in the 18th century and dug many of Sindh's irrigation canals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kalhoras were a family of hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> leaders in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> whose followers formed an armed movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mian-yar-muhammad-kalhoro">Mian Yar Muhammad Kalhoro</a> was recognised by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> as governor of parts of Sindh around 1701, and his successors extended their rule over the whole of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mian-nur-muhammad-kalhoro">Mian Nur Muhammad Kalhoro</a>, the most powerful ruler, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nadir Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>, paying heavy tribute. The Kalhoras built canals and founded towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khudabad">Khudabad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>. The great Sindhi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-abdul-latif-bhittai">Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai</a> lived under their rule.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rivalry between the Kalhora rulers and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> Talpur commanders led to civil war after a Talpur leader was murdered. The Talpurs defeated the last Kalhora ruler, Abdul Nabi, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halani">Battle of Halani</a> in 1783 and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/talpur-dynasty">Talpur dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalhora-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kalhora dynasty" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: PavelD · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1701 CE – 1783 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khudabad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mian Yar Muhammad Kalhoro, Mian Nur Muhammad Kalhoro, Mian Ghulam Shah Kalhoro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sindhi, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kalhoras, Kalhora State of Sindh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=kalhora-dynasty" aria-label="Kalhora dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6460 1209 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalhora dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6780 1465L7018 1385M6780 1465L6757 1183M6780 1465L6811 1500"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M6757 1183h0M6811 1500h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6780 1465h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6780 1465h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khudabad. <a href="/atlas?year=1742&amp;focus=kalhora-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
